# /mnt/jffs2 is the one writable persistent partition: QSPI mtd2 ("qspi-nvmfs").
# hw_serial is minted there once and must survive every reflash, so this is
# mounted rw and NEVER reformatted. noatime because jffs2 on NOR flash has a
# write budget and atime spends it for nothing.
/dev/mtdblock2  /mnt/jffs2  jffs2  rw,noatime  0 0

# The boot partition, so uImage and the device tree are reachable from the
# running system - which is how tools/flash.sh updates them over the network.
# It is what the Zynq BootROM reads BOOT.bin from, hence FAT.
LABEL=FISHBOOT  /boot       vfat   defaults,noatime  0 2

# The root. noatime because this is a consumer SD card and the Buildroot rootfs it
# replaces wrote to the card NEVER, being a ramdisk - so write volume is the one
# genuine durability cost of moving to Debian.
#
# commit=30, NOT commit=600, and that number was paid for. With 600 the kernel
# holds metadata and data in memory for up to TEN MINUTES, so a hard power cycle
# silently discards everything written in that window. It discarded a set of
# systemd units and three scripts that had been deployed, verified running, and
# `systemctl enable`d minutes earlier: the filesystem came back "clean" because
# the journal had nothing to replay - the writes had simply never reached the
# card - and the board booted with no USB gadget and therefore no way in.
#
# 30 s bounds the loss to something a person would notice losing, and the wear
# difference against 600 is negligible next to journald, which is capped
# separately. If you deploy something you care about, `sync` anyway.
LABEL=fishroot  /           ext4   defaults,noatime,commit=30,errors=remount-ro  0 1
